      "id": 1,
      "text": "West Sacramento was incorporated in 1987 from three former towns: Broderick, Bryte, and the original West Sacramento community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Sacramento%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city sits on the west bank of the Sacramento River, directly opposite Sacramento, separating Yolo and Sacramento counties.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Sacramento%2C_California",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1844, Flemish traveler John Schwartz was the first Euro‑American to settle there, building a shack six miles south of the river confluence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Sacramento%2C_California",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The area that became Broderick was once called the Town of Washington before being renamed in honor of a local pioneer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/West_Sacramento%2C_California",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "West Sacramento hosts the CBS television station KOVR, whose studios are located on KOVR Drive within the city.",
